  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 152 PM MDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nHigh pressure will reposition toward the AZ-NM border, south of\nI-40 on Wednesday with heights reducing by a couple of decameters.\nTuesday's storms will have sent moist outflows westward beyond the\nRio Grande while the backdoor frontal airmass continues to hold\nground over the southern plains. This scenario will continue to \nprovide a favorable environment for strong to severe storms, just\nabout anywhere east of the central mountain chain on Wednesday.\nThe latest REFS QPF mean is boasting some heftier rainfall totals\nnear and just east of the central mountain chain while the \nContinental Divide also is shown to host more storms Wednesday. \n\nWednesday's convection will likely be the main determinant for\nThursday's activity, meaning the extent of moisture from \nWednesday outflows and the subsequent wind components will dictate\nthe most likely areas to observe convective initiation. The\nbackdoor frontal boundary does look like it might start to relax \nand wash out in NM late in Thursday, but if the upslope \nsoutheasterly flow east of the central mountain chain can survive,\na healthier dose of storms will follow. West central to\nsouthwestern areas should still observe a healthy coverage of\nstorms on Thursday too, even with the upper high being stronger \nin that area. \n\nThe high would potentially begin to nudge back northeastward\nslightly on Friday with storm coverage being a bit more subdued\ndue to reliance on moisture recycling. Into the weekend, tropical\nstorm activity continues to be modeled in the far eastern Pacific\nwith westward headings that will pose no direct impacts to NM.\nHowever, the upper high does appear to sidestep eastward. This\nwould open the door to southwesterlies aloft that could yield a \nmore traditional monsoon plume of moisture over eastern AZ and\nwestern NM. Ensemble means are supportive of this as well with all\nclusters showing anomalously high PWATs entering much of the\nnorthwestern half of NM through the weekend. Conceptually this\nmakes sense, as the jet does tend to dip farther south in\nresponse to more ITCZ or tropical convection, but we'll \ndefinitely want more run-to-run continuity before getting \noverzealous.",
